{"collected_at": "2026-11-11T19:23:06Z", "deployment_id": null, "error": null, "finish_reason": "stop", "input_tokens": 35, "latency_ms": 19449, "max_tokens": 1200, "model_digest": null, "model_key": "gpt-6.1-terra", "output_tokens": 758, "persona_id": "P13", "prompt_category": null, "prompt_group": "v1_creative", "prompt_id": "V1-15", "prompt_set_version": "fingerprint_v0", "provider": "openai", "quantization": null, "reasoning_text": null, "record_id": "9db4629f-889b-43f1-8f99-1e19e88d4947", "refused": false, "requested_model": "gpt-6.1-terra", "response_text": "A realistic “zombie apocalypse” would involve **living people with severe neurological illness**, not reanimated corpses. A virus can disrupt behavior, movement and judgment, but it cannot restart a dead brain or make a body function without oxygen, water and energy.\n\nRabies offers a loose biological analogy: it affects the nervous system and can cause agitation, confusion and difficulty swallowing. But real rabies usually has a long incubation period and is not efficiently spread by casual contact. An illness that makes people violently confused would also tend to incapacitate them, limiting their ability to travel and transmit it. A global collapse is therefore possible as fiction, but unlikely from a rabies-like outbreak alone.\n\n### How it might unfold\n\n1. **Detection:** Hospitals in several connected communities report unusual cases of fever, confusion and aggression. Early reports are mistaken for drug intoxication, other infections or unrelated violence. Health officials investigate, while rumors spread faster than confirmed information.\n\n2. **Containment:** Authorities identify how the illness spreads and issue guidance. Depending on the route of transmission, measures might include isolating sick patients, tracing contacts and limiting some gatherings. Hospital crowding and staff shortages strain routine care. If transmission requires bites, containment is more plausible than in a scenario involving efficient transmission before symptoms.\n\n3. **Disruption:** If cases continue rising, fear and absenteeism interrupt transport, deliveries and some public services. Local shortages develop. Misinformation and distrust make cooperation harder, while communities and health workers organize support. Infrastructure would not necessarily fail everywhere at once.\n\n4. **Divergence and recovery:** Some areas contain the outbreak; others struggle with overstretched services and displacement. If transmission falls, societies gradually restore supplies and healthcare, investigate what worked, and care for survivors. Prolonged collapse would require compounding problems—such as widespread illness among essential workers and breakdowns in public trust.\n\n### Survival strategies\n\n- **Follow verified local health guidance.** The right precautions depend on whether infection spreads through bites, close contact or another route.\n- **Avoid injured or aggressive people at close range**, but don’t assume every confused person is infected. Contact emergency services when possible.\n- **Treat bites promptly:** Wash the wound thoroughly with soap and running water and seek urgent medical advice. Real animal bites may require rabies prevention.\n- **Prepare for short-term disruption:** Keep drinking water, shelf-stable food, essential medication, a first-aid kit, a flashlight and a battery-powered radio.\n- **Stay connected:** Coordinate with trusted neighbors, check on vulnerable people and share reliable information. Avoid unnecessary travel and crowded locations when officials advise it.\n- **Protect basic health:** Safe water, sanitation, sleep and access to medical care matter more than dramatic confrontations.\n\nThe most plausible crisis would resemble a severe public-health emergency with episodes of violence—not an endless horde of tireless undead.", "run_type": "library", "sample_index": 0, "seed": null, "served_model": "gpt-6.1-terra", "tag": null, "temperature": null}
